<div class="prose max-w-prose">

As part of a group of friends who host a monthly meetup, I created the route,
drew the artwork, designed and printed the maps, and created custom sticker
sheets for the Bristol Stationery Crawl.

This was a community-led project rooted in creativity, local discovery, and
shared experience. The goal was to create a fun and memorable way to guide
people through a curated route while also reinforcing the personality of the
event itself.

## The concept

The Bristol Stationery Crawl is designed to bring together stationery lovers and
creative people in a relaxed, social format. I wanted the experience to feel
playful, thoughtful, and distinctly Bristol — with a route that felt easy to
follow while still having a strong sense of character.

I created the route structure, designed the visual language, and developed the
printed materials that people would use during the crawl itself.

## The design work

This project involved a range of design tasks: route planning, illustration,
print design, and small-batch production. I created the artwork and mapping for
the event, designing materials that were practical enough to use on the day but
still visually engaging and memorable.

The printed maps were one of the most important pieces, because they needed to
be clear, legible, and easy to navigate while still feeling like part of a
cohesive event identity. I also created sticker sheets that were printed and
cut in-house, adding an extra tactile element to the experience and helping
strengthen the event branding.

## Production

A key part of the project was making it work in the real world. The materials
needed to be designed for production and small-batch printing, while still
feeling considered and high quality. I handled the full process from design to
print, including creating the artwork, preparing files for production, and
cutting the sticker sheets myself.

This meant balancing creativity with practicality — ensuring the final pieces
were enjoyable to use, easy to reproduce, and aligned with the atmosphere of the
meetup.

## Why it mattered

Projects like this are important because they combine personal creativity with
community building. The Bristol Stationery Crawl was not just about producing
materials — it was about creating a shared experience that people could follow,
collect, and enjoy.

It also reinforced my approach to design as something that can be both playful
and functional: visually distinctive, but still useful in the real world.

